     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi everyone, <div><br></div><div>Hopefully this isn&#39;t a redundant topic but I haven&#39;t been able to find a previous thread that addresses it. I am using dynamic n-mixture models to estimate tadpole density and survival over the course of the summer. Because there shouldn&#39;t be any recruitment over my trapping period, I fixed the gamma parameter:</div><div><br></div><div><div>am5 &lt;- pcountOpen(~WetlandType+ElevationM+Year, ~1, ~1, ~offset(log(numTraps)) , AmtiAbundUmf, K=50,fix= &quot;gamma&quot;, mixture=&quot;ZIP&quot;)</div></div><div><br></div><div>When I try to evaluate goodness of fit using the Nmix.gof.test or parboot functions I get the error:</div><div><div><br></div><div>Nmix.gof.test(am5, nsim = 50)</div></div><div>&quot;Error in Xgam %*% coef(object, &quot;gamma&quot;) : requires numeric/complex matrix/vector arguments&quot;<br></div><div><br></div><div>Does anyone know of a way to get around this problem? These functions work just fine when I allow gamma to be estimated. </div><div><br></div><div>If not, any thoughts on whether I can evaluate goodness of fit with gamma in the model?</div><div><br></div><div>Thank you!</div><span><font color=\"#888888\"><div><br></div></font></span></div></blockquote></div></div>"
